ich is year-round, is a consistent direct option \ue200cite\ue202turn0news14\ue202turn0search10\ue201.\n\n---\n\n##  Best Option: Edinburgh\n\n### **Why Edinburgh edges out Glasgow**:\n1. **Direct flight to NYC**—dramatically decreases travel risk for your Jan 21–22 arrival.\n2. **More airline options** (Delta, JetBlue, United) → greater scheduling flexibility.\n3. **Potential lower prices** in January.\n\n### **Sample Itinerary (Multi-City/Open-Jaw Ticket)**:\n- **Jan 13**: SAN → Edinburgh (via ORD/BOS/AMS/LHR).\n- **Jan 21 or early Jan 22**: Edinburgh → New York (nonstop, ~7½–8 h).\n- **Jan 25**: New York → San Diego (nonstop).\n\nThat structure prioritizes your key arrival date in NYC and maximizes reliability.\n\n---\n\n##  Booking Tip: Multi-City vs. Separate Tickets\n\nUse a **multi-city (open-jaw)** itinerary—one ticket with:\n- SAN → Edinburgh  \n- Edinburgh → NYC  \n- NYC → SAN\n\nThis ensures:\n- **Protected connections** across all legs.\n- **Baggage through-checked** across flights.\n- Fewer logistical or delay issues.\n\n(Open-jaw tickets are standard for such routing where origin/destination vary) \ue200cite\ue202turn0search20\ue201.\n\n---\n\n##  Summary Table\n\n| Route Segment            | Edinburgh (EDI)                        | Glasgow (GLA)                         |\n|--------------------------|----------------------------------------|----------------------------------------|\n| SAN → UK                | 1 stop, ~14h via major hubs            | 1 stop, ~11.5–12h via AMS or LHR       |\n| UK → NYC (Jan 21–22)     | **Nonstop available** (Delta, JetBlue, United) | **No nonstop**; requires stop         |\n| Booking Convenience      | High — direct leg increases reliability | Lower — extra connection adds risk     |\n| Best for Your Constraint | **Yes**                                | Not recommended                        |\n\n---\n\n###  Final Recommendation\n\nGo with **Edinburgh**. Book it as a **single multi-city ticket**:\n- **SAN → Edinburgh*