our companies** that run these 3-day routes, or do you prefer a **self-drive itinerary**?']
Title: UK TV licence explained
Author: user
Message: ['Explain the uk tv license ']
Author: assistant
Message: ['The **UK TV licence** is a type of household fee that helps fund the BBC. Here’s how it works:  \n\n### 📺 What it is\n- The **TV Licence** is basically a legal permit you need in the UK if you want to **watch or record live TV** on any channel (not just BBC) or **watch BBC programmes on iPlayer** (live or on-demand).  \n- It applies whether you’re watching on a **TV set, laptop, tablet, phone, or streaming device**.  \n\n### 💰 Cost\n- As of **2025**, the standard licence costs **£169.50 per year** for a colour TV licence (black and white is cheaper but almost nobody uses that anymore).  \n- You can pay all at once or in instalments.  \n\n### 👩\u200d👩\u200d👧 Who needs it\nYou need a TV licence if you:\n- Watch or record TV as it’s being broadcast (on Freeview, Sky, Virgin, streaming, or even YouTube if it’s a live broadcast).  \n- Use **BBC iPlayer** (live or catch-up).  \n\nYou **do not need** one if you:\n- Only watch streaming services like **Netflix, Disney+, Prime Video, or other on-demand platforms** that don’t include live TV.  \n- Only watch DVDs or play video games.  \n\n### 🏡 One per household\n- One licence covers an entire household (everyone living at the same address).  \n- Students may need their own if they watch TV in student housing, unless they’re covered by their parents’ licence under certain conditions.  \n\n### 📢 Enforcement\n- TV Licensing (the agency that manages this) sends out letters and may send inspectors to check.  \n- If you’re caught watching live TV or iPlayer without a licence, you can be fined up to **£1,000**.  \n\n### 🔑 Why it exists\n- The money funds the BBC’s TV, radio, online services, and educational content.  \n- The idea is that the BBC stays free from advertising and political/commercial influence because it’s funded directly by the public.  \n\n👉 Do you want me to also explain **why it’s so controversial** and how people in the UK feel about paying it?']
