Message: ['I\'m not entirely sure what "a third DRO" refers to — "DRO" can mean different things depending on context. Here are two common uses:\n\n---\n\n###  **1. Debt Relief Order (UK)**\nA **Debt Relief Order (DRO)** is a formal insolvency tool in the UK, designed for individuals with relatively low debt, low surplus income, and few assets. It offers a quicker, lower-cost alternative to bankruptcy by granting debt relief over 12 months \ue200cite\ue202turn0search18\ue202turn0search14\ue201.\n\nIf you\'re asking whether a *third DRO* is planned or permitted for government employees or anyone else, there\'s no such designation or "third DRO" as a distinct category of debt relief currently recognized in UK law—or any announced changes to introduce one.\n\n---\n\n###  **2. Domestic Relations Order (DRO in Pension/Retirement context)**\nIn retirement and divorce contexts, particularly in the U.S., a **Domestic Relations Order (DRO)**—specifically a **Qualified Domestic Relations Order (QDRO)** under ERISA—is used to assign part of a retirement plan to a former spouse or dependent after divorce \ue200cite\ue202turn0search1\ue202turn0search13\ue202turn0search17\ue201.\n\nIf you\'re asking whether a **third** such order is being introduced or required for government employees—there’s no indication of any new third-tier DRO in this context.\n\n---\n\n###  Could you clarify a bit more?\n- Are you referring to a debt-related DRO (like bankruptcy tools in the UK)?\n- Or a retirement/pension-related DRO (like QDROs for benefit division)?\n- Or possibly something else entirely?\n\nIf you give me a little more detail on the context or jurisdiction you\'re thinking about, I’d be happy to dig deeper and help you find the answer.']
