erms and deadlines**, which gave more certainty compared to waiting for possible **RIFs (reductions in force)**, which often come with less control and more stress.  \n\n---\n\n## 📋 Example Scenario  \nSay an Army civilian making $80,000/year is approved for DRP 3.0:\n- They stop working September 1 but keep full pay/benefits until December 31 → that’s **about $26,500 in paid leave** while job-hunting or preparing for retirement.\n- They resign voluntarily December 31 → no RIF mark, clean separation.\n- If retirement-eligible → they roll straight into pension/benefits with no gap in pay.  \n\n---\n\n👉 In short:  \nThe **main benefit** of DRP 3.0 was **getting paid for up to four months without working** while securing a voluntary, stigma-free exit — often with the added bonus of bridging into retirement or new employment.  \n\n---\n\nDo you want me to also compare these benefits against what might happen if someone *didn’t* take DRP 3.0 (e.g., risk of RIF or reassignment)?']
