Message: ['Good question — years of service were a **big factor** in how much someone benefited from taking **DRP 3.0**. Let me break it down:\n\n---\n\n## 🕑 How **Years of Service** Worked Under DRP 3.0\n\n### 1. **Creditable Service Continued During DRP Leave**\n- While on **paid administrative leave (Sept–Dec 2025)**, employees **kept earning creditable service time** toward:\n  - **Retirement eligibility** (Minimum Retirement Age + Years of Service, or 20/30-year thresholds),\n  - **High-3 average salary calculation** (if those months fell in their highest-paid years),\n  - **Annual leave and sick leave accrual** (until final separation).  \n\n➡️ This was valuable if someone was just shy of meeting retirement eligibility — those months could **push them over the line**.\n\n---\n\n### 2. **Eligibility at Separation (Dec 31, 2025)**\n- To **retire under DRP 3.0**, an employee had to already **meet eligibility requirements** (or reach them by Dec 31, 2025).  \n- If they weren’t eligible to retire, they could still **resign voluntarily**, but they wouldn’t get an **immediate annuity** — instead, they might qualify for a **deferred or postponed retirement** later, depending on their years of service.  \n\n---\n\n### 3. **Impact Based on Service Length**\n- **Long-service employees (20–30+ years):**\n  - Could often retire directly from DRP with **full benefits**.\n  - DRP was essentially a paid “glide path” into retirement.  \n- **Mid-career employees (10–19 years):**\n  - DRP gave them income and creditable service time, but many would **resign instead of retire**. They’d keep credit for their service and could claim **deferred retirement** later (typically at age 60 or their MRA, depending on the system — FERS vs. CSRS).  \n- **Early-career employees (<10 years):**\n  - Could resign under DRP and keep their **FERS contributions and service credit** for a deferred pension later, but **no immediate benefits**.  \n  - Still beneficial because they got several months of **paid time** to plan their next move.\n\n---\n\n### 4. **No Years-of-Service Requirement to Participate**\n- Unlike some retirement incentives (like VSIP or early-out VERA programs), **DRP 3.0 didn’t require a minimum number of service years** to be eligible.  \n- The only requirement was that you had to be in a **surplus position** (or encumbering one).  \n\n---\n\n✅ **Bottom Line:**  \n- **Any years of service counted** during DRP leave.  \n- The **biggest winners** were those close to retirement eligibility — because DRP 3.0 let them finish out their service time on **paid leave** and then step straight into retirement.  \n\n---\n\nWould you like me to map out the **retirement scenarios (immediate, deferred, postponed)** based on years of service + age, to show exactly how DRP 3.0 participants might have exited?']
Title: PhD in Organizational Leadership
